        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <div class="honest-take">
    <p class="honest-take__label">Two stages of competition</p>
    <p>
      Entry looks easy: register and start. But there is a second, harder filter later. Clients give a large sum only to someone they trust, so the real competition is for repeat clients and referrals. In Maharashtra, about 47,000 agents had registered by 2024, yet only about 13,000 were left with valid registration after cancellations and suspensions. Registering is the first filter. Staying compliant and trusted is the second. Those removals were for paperwork, so they say nothing about who earned well.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="ownership">The ownership layers</h2>

  <p>
    Real estate has more ownership routes than most careers, because a business here can be run from a phone, a sheet and trust. Each layer is a business, not a job title.
  </p>

  <div class="highlight-box highlight-box--teal">
    <p>
      <strong>Why these layers scale.</strong> A property management firm earns a repeat monthly fee from each owner it serves, and a trained team can serve more owners than you can alone. A brokerage with written steps and a shared listing record lets a new agent do the work the way you do it. A marketing service sells one skill to many developers. In each case you set the price, you can hire, and income does not stop when your own hours stop.
    </p>
    <p>
      A fair warning about "ownership over salary": owning a business built on repeat clients is different from buying a flat with a loan. The first grows with skill and trust. The second grows with capital and the market, and a loan adds risk. Do not confuse the two.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="comparison-grid">
    <div class="compare-card compare-card--teal">
      <div class="compare-card__label">What the layer needs</div>
      <ul>
        <li>Written steps for every repeated job (viewing, paperwork, move-in checks).</li>
        <li>A lead and deal sheet that anyone on the team can read.</li>
        <li>Your firm's own registration, GST if you cross the limit, and agreements in writing.</li>
        <li>One trained person who can do your job when you are not there.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div class="compare-card compare-card--gold">
      <div class="compare-card__label">What goes wrong</div>
      <ul>
        <li>Hiring agents before your own process works, so errors multiply.</li>
        <li>Depending on one developer or one portal for most leads.</li>
        <li>Mixing client money with personal money. Keep them apart from day one.</li>
        <li>Taking on repair and tenant liability without a clear contract.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2 id="rera">RERA registration, step by step</h2>

  <p>
    The Real Estate (Regulation and Development) Act, 2016 requires an agent to register before facilitating the sale or purchase of a unit in a registered project. One registration number is issued for each state or Union Territory, and you quote it in every sale. Registration is by state, so the portal of your state authority is the final word.
  </p>

